This graph converts inches ($x$) to centimetres ($y$).
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.6]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (0,0) grid (6,6);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(6.5,0) node[right,font=\scriptsize]{in};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,6.5) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{cm};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1,0) {$2$};\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (2,0) {$4$};\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3,0) {$6$};\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4,0) {$8$};\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (5,0) {$10$};\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (6,0) {$12$};
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,1) {$5$};\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,2) {$10$};\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,3) {$15$};\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,4) {$20$};\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,5) {$25$};\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,6) {$30$};
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--(6,6);


\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Use the graph to convert $8$ inches to centimetres.

This graph uses a hypothetical simplified model of $4$ litres per gallon to convert litres ($x$) to gallons ($y$); it is not a physical gallon conversion.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.6]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (0,0) grid (6,6);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(6.5,0) node[right,font=\scriptsize]{L};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,6.5) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{gal};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1,0) {$6$};\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (2,0) {$12$};\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3,0) {$18$};\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4,0) {$24$};\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (5,0) {$30$};\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (6,0) {$36$};
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,1) {$1.5$};\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,2) {$3$};\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,3) {$4.5$};\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,4) {$6$};\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,5) {$7.5$};\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,6) {$9$};
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--(6,6);


\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Use the graph to convert $6$ gallons to litres.

This graph converts Australian dollars, AUD ($x$), to New Zealand dollars, NZD ($y$).
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.6]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (0,0) grid (6,6);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(6.5,0) node[right,font=\scriptsize]{AUD};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,6.5) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{NZD};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1,0) {$10$};\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (2,0) {$20$};\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3,0) {$30$};\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4,0) {$40$};\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (5,0) {$50$};\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (6,0) {$60$};
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,1) {$11$};\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,2) {$22$};\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,3) {$33$};\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,4) {$44$};\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,5) {$55$};\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,6) {$66$};
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--(6,6);


\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Which equation relates NZD ($y$) and AUD ($x$)?

A conversion graph relates miles per hour ($M$) and kilometres per hour ($K$). Its line passes through the origin and the labelled point $(25,40)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.6]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(6.5,0) node[right] {$M$ (mph)};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,5) node[above] {$K$ (km/h)};
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--(6,4.8);
\fill (5,4) circle (2.2pt);
\node[above left] at (5,4) {$(25,40)$};
\node[below left] at (0,0) {$0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Use the labelled point to find $k$ in $K = kM$.

Two graphs, $A$ and $B$, are shown below.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\begin{scope}
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (0,0) grid (6,6);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(6.5,0) node[right,font=\scriptsize]{L};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,6.5) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{gal};
\draw[thick,red] (0,0)--(6,6);
\fill (0,0) circle (2.2pt);
\node[font=\small] at (3,-1.3) {$A$: litres to gallons};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(8,0)}]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (0,0) grid (6,6);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(6.5,0) node[right,font=\scriptsize]{$^{\circ}$C};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,6.5) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{$^{\circ}$F};
\draw[thick,blue] (0,2.13)--(6,5.73);
\fill (0,2.13) circle (2.2pt);
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,2.13) {$32$};
\node[font=\small] at (3,-1.3) {$B$: Celsius to Fahrenheit};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Which graph could be used to convert litres to gallons using direct variation reasoning (that is, $0$ units of one equals $0$ of the other)?

